\ ff-test: lines
\ `execute` runs every kind of word from inside the dispatch loop.
\
\ It used to call back into the interpreter in C for each word, so errors
\ inside escaped the caller and deep recursion used up the C stack. It
\ now enters the word's executable stub under a return frame, as compiled
\ code calls a word. This covers each shape of stub: primitive, colon
\ definition, constant, variable, array, created and DOES> words, a
\ deferred word, an immediate word, and the internals such as `branch`
\ whose inline operand a stub can't supply (they now do nothing, rather
\ than jumping off into memory).

: p 32 emit . ;
: sq dup * ;
10 constant ten
variable v
5 array arr
create buf 2 allot
: const create , does> @ ;
42 const answer
defer hook
' sq is hook

3 ' dup execute p p cr
7 ' sq execute p cr
' ten execute p cr
99 ' v execute ! v @ p cr
123 2 ' arr execute ! 2 arr @ p cr
' buf execute buf = p cr
' answer execute p cr
6 ' hook execute p cr
: runner execute ;
4 ' sq runner p cr
' ten ' execute execute p cr
: tp ['] .( execute ;
tp "direct" cr
5 ' literal execute
clear ' (strlit) execute ' branch execute depth p cr

\ Nor can those be compiled by name. With no operand of their own they
\ took the next word's code for one: `(xdo)` pushed a loop that the
\ return then took for its frame, `branch` jumped by an opcode.
: bad1 (lit) ;
: bad2 3 (xdo) . ;
: bad3 branch dup ;
: bad4 postpone (strlit) ; immediate
: use bad4 ;
